@page "/kretzmann"
@using BibleAtlas.Client.Contracts
@using BibleAtlas.Client.Explore
@using BibleAtlas.Client.State
@using BibleAtlas.Client.Views
@implements IAsyncDisposable
@inject StateAtom<Locus> LocusAtom
@inject StateAtom<ViewArrangement> ViewArrangementAtom
@inject AtlasClient Atlas
@inject IExplorableClient Graph
@inject ViewRegistry Registry
@inject IJSRuntime JS

@* Batch CORPREAD-1b (ticket K, owner order verbatim: "we should have the
   whole kretzmann commentary just like on the kretzmann.org, just having
   the verses be clickable and explorable ... we need an escape hatch to
   stop following the reader as well ... design it like a sensible UI
   developer would") -- REBUILDS CORP-1's own item-list/card browser
   (batch-corp1-report.md §4) into a CONTINUOUS READING SURFACE: the
   chapter's own commentary, in canonical order, flowing as prose (a quiet
   verse-anchor label, then that verse's own commentary item(s) as ordinary
   paragraphs) -- the SAME reading-column register Reader.razor already
   establishes (.reader-column typography, reused, not re-invented), not a
   list of bordered cards.

   FIX ROUND (S-1, CRITICAL -- review): the first ship of this batch
   DISCARDED the corpus's own section headings the instant an item's prose
   resolved (`@if (item.Text is { } text) { <ScriptureRefText .../> } else
   { @item.Heading ... }` -- a heading rendered ONLY while loading, then
   gone forever), which is the exact opposite of kretzmann.org's own
   defining structure (a bolded section heading above each block of verse
   commentary) and of the owner's own words ("just like on the
   kretzmann.org"). Fixed the same way `Concord.RenderRows` already
   de-duplicates part/article headings: `LoadCommentaryAsync` below computes
   `CommentaryItemRow.IsSectionStart` ONCE, structurally, the instant an
   item's own Heading differs from the immediately preceding item's
   (canonical order, across verse boundaries too) -- a `kretzmann-section-
   heading-{slug}` row then renders WITH the prose, ALWAYS, whether that
   item's own text has arrived yet or not (S-C1: "renders section headings
   WITH prose -- both, always").

   FOLLOW/RELEASE (deliverable 0a/0b, THE FOLLOW-RELEASE LAW): this view
   still declares ViewCapabilities.BearsLocus and, BY DEFAULT, still
   projects the shared Locus atom directly (`Following` below, true unless
   BOTH split-open AND the shared ViewArrangement's own Follow flag is
   false) -- CORP-1's own "split-follow-by-construction" proof is UNCHANGED
   for the default (following) case: no link, no held copy, both panes
   simply render the SAME atom. The NEW toggle-follow chip (below, rendered
   only while split -- mirrors World.razor's own follow-chip, which
   likewise only renders while SplitMode) invokes the SAME declared
   ToggleFollowHatch every locus-bearing view now carries
   (ViewRegistrySetup.cs) -- flips the ONE shared ViewArrangement.Follow
   flag (client/State/ViewArrangement.cs's own pre-existing ToggleFollow
   intent, reused verbatim, not reinvented). RELEASED: `_releasedBook`/
   `_releasedChapter` (plain scalar fields -- NOT typed as Locus, so this
   deliberately does NOT trip ConformanceTests.cs's own no-copy scan, the
   SAME "view-local plain-scalar position, not an atom copy" shape ST-3's
   own D2 ruling already ledgers for Concord.razor's `_currentRef`) seed
   from the shared value at the INSTANT of release and this view then
   browses independently (picker/prev-next write the view-local fields,
   never LocusAtom, while released) until re-follow reconverges (drops the
   seed, reloads from whatever the shared atom holds NOW -- the brief's own
   "SyncToken discipline": re-follow always snaps to CURRENT shared state,
   never a stale remembered one).

   VERSE REFS EXPLORABLE (ticket K): every commentary paragraph's own prose
   renders through the shared Components/ScriptureRefText.razor scanner
   (Explore/ScriptureRefScan.cs) -- the SAME mechanism Concord.razor uses
   (the brief's own "do not fork the scanner"); a verse ANCHOR (the small
   marginal label opening each verse's own block) is independently
   explorable too (a plain VerseNode push, ONE-RULE).

   Body data: LISTING (KRETZ-SCALE-1, unchanged -- one chapter-scoped
   request, `AtlasClient.KretzmannChapter`) carries only each item's own
   HEADING; the FULL PROSE (CORP-1b's own additive `description` field on
   the generic node card) is fetched per item, LAZILY, one item at a time as
   its own row approaches the viewport.

   FIX ROUND (Q-2, CRITICAL -- review, KRETZMANN-13): the first ship of this
   batch fetched EVERY item's own prose CONCURRENTLY the instant the listing
   resolved (`Task.WhenAll` over the whole chapter) -- reintroducing
   KRETZ-SCALE-1's own retired per-verse fan-out through `Card()` instead of
   the retired `.../edges?kind=commented-on-by` shape, with its own guard
   test narrowed to assert only the OLD url stayed at zero rather than that
   the NEW one was bounded ("never do anything like this," controller
   ruling). Fixed for real: `wwwroot/js/lazyProse.js`'s own
   IntersectionObserver requests an item's own prose only once its row
   approaches the viewport (a generous rootMargin, so the fetch starts
   slightly ahead of scroll); `OnItemNearViewport` below additionally gates
   ACTUAL CONCURRENT fetches through a `SemaphoreSlim(8)`. KRETZMANN-13
   (tests/ux/kretzmann.spec.ts) now asserts a genuine TOTAL-request bound on
   PSA.119 (176 verses) -- some prose loads without scrolling (whatever is
   near the viewport), but nowhere near all of it.

   KRETZ-PROSE-SCALE (PARKED, disclosed, not built this round): the REAL fix
   is a future, additive server change -- widen the chapter-scoped LISTING
   response to carry each item's own prose, collapsing this whole mechanism
   back to KRETZ-SCALE-1's original ONE-request shape. Zero server changes
   is this batch's own standing machine rule, so this ticket stays parked;
   the lazy-load mitigation above is the honest, disclosed interim fix, not
   a stand-in for the real one.

   FIX ROUND 2 (re-review finding, IMPORTANT -- CompositionSplit.razor's own
   RULING 4(a), the VC-1 hazard class): the lazy-load observer used to
   rebind only on a NEW chapter load (a `_pendingObserve` flag), which
   silently orphaned it on a same-chapter split toggle (CompositionSplit
   discards/recreates every ChildContent DOM node across that transition,
   per its own header) -- any not-yet-requested item's prose then never
   loaded again until the reader changed chapters. Fixed: `OnAfterRenderAsync`
   below now rebinds UNCONDITIONALLY, every render, the same "bind on every
   render, rely on the JS side's own idempotency" discipline `Reader.razor`'s
   `watchScroll`/`watchChapterNavCenter` already establish for this exact
   hazard class -- see that method's own comment for the full reasoning. *@

<PageTitle>Bible Explorer &mdash; Kretzmann</PageTitle>

<CompositionSplit HostName="@ViewNames.Kretzmann" SplitModeOverride="@SplitMode" HostPaneClass="split-pane-host"
                   OnArrangementChanged="OnArrangementChanged" @ref="_compositionSplit" Context="ctx">
    <div class="@($"kretzmann-page{ctx.HostPaneClassSuffix}")" data-testid="kretzmann-page">
        <article class="kretzmann-column reader-column">
            <h1 class="kretzmann-title">Kretzmann</h1>
            <p class="kretzmann-intro">
                Paul E. Kretzmann's <em>Popular Commentary of the Bible</em>
                (Concordia Publishing House, 1921&ndash;1924) &mdash; verse by
                verse, subordinate to the text it serves.
            </p>

            <div class="kretzmann-controls">
                <ScripturePicker OnApply="ApplyScriptureRef" ThemeClass="picker-parchment" CurrentBook="@_book" CurrentChapter="@_chapterNum" />

                @if (!ctx.IsSplitOpen)
                {
                    <button type="button" class="split-open split-open-parchment" data-testid="split-open-kretzmann"
                            @onclick="ctx.InvokeHatch">
                        Read beside the reader
                    </button>
                }
                else
                {
                    @* Batch CORPREAD-1b, deliverable 0a/ticket K: the
                       release/re-follow chip -- rendered ONLY while split
                       (mirrors World.razor's own follow-chip, which
                       likewise only exists while SplitMode; there is
                       nothing to escape FROM while standalone -- see this
                       file's own header).
                       Fix round (Q-3, IMPORTANT -- review): `.follow-chip`
                       alone is World's own DUSK register (dark glass over
                       the night map) -- reused verbatim here put a dark
                       chip on Kretzmann's own PARCHMENT page, directly
                       contradicting the register law and the report's own
                       "no new colors" claim. `.follow-chip-parchment`
                       (app.css) is the SAME modifier idiom
                       `.split-open-parchment`/`.picker-parchment` already
                       establish for "this control, on a parchment page." *@
                    <button type="button" class="follow-chip follow-chip-parchment" data-testid="kretzmann-follow-chip"
                            aria-pressed="@(Following ? "true" : "false")" @onclick="ToggleFollowClick">
                        @(Following ? "Following the reader" : "Reading independently")
                    </button>
                }
            </div>

            <h2 class="chapter-head kretzmann-chapter-head" data-testid="kretzmann-chapter-head">
                <span class="chapter-head-book">@BookName</span>
                <span class="chapter-head-num">@_chapterNum</span>
            </h2>

            @if (_error is not null)
            {
                <div class="toast" data-testid="toast">
                    <p class="toast-message">@_error</p>
                </div>
            }
            else if (_loading)
            {
                <p class="kretzmann-loading">Loading&hellip;</p>
            }
            else if (_rows.Count == 0)
            {
                <p class="kretzmann-empty" data-testid="kretzmann-empty">No commentary is indexed for this chapter.</p>
            }
            else
            {
                @foreach (var group in _rows)
                {
                    <section class="kretzmann-verse-block" data-testid="@($"kretzmann-verse-group-{group.Verse}")">
                        <button type="button" class="kretzmann-verse-anchor explorable-quiet" data-testid="@($"kretzmann-verse-anchor-{group.Verse}")"
                                aria-label="@($"Explore {BookName} {_chapterNum}:{group.Verse}")"
                                @onclick="@(() => OnExploreVerse(group.Verse))">
                            @group.Verse
                        </button>
                        @foreach (var item in group.Items)
                        {
                            @* Fix round (S-1, CRITICAL -- review): the section
                               heading renders WITH the prose, ALWAYS -- while
                               loading and after -- never discarded once text
                               arrives (S-C1). IsSectionStart is computed once,
                               structurally, in LoadCommentaryAsync -- not a
                               markup-time dedup. *@
                            @if (item.IsSectionStart)
                            {
                                <h3 class="kretzmann-section-heading" data-testid="@($"kretzmann-section-heading-{Slug(item.Id)}")">@item.Heading</h3>
                            }
                            @* Fix round (Q-6, IMPORTANT -- review): the
                               `aria-label="Explore ..."` this wrapper used to
                               carry REPLACED its accessible name -- a screen
                               reader announced only "Explore <heading>,
                               button" and never read the paragraph itself.
                               Dropped; the element's own text content is now
                               its accessible name (still focusable/actionable
                               via tabindex+Enter, same as before).
                               data-kretzmann-item-id/data-loaded (Q-2): the
                               lazy-load observer's own hook -- see this
                               file's own header and wwwroot/js/lazyProse.js. *@
                            <div class="kretzmann-item explorable" data-testid="@($"kretzmann-item-{Slug(item.Id)}")"
                                 data-kretzmann-item-id="@item.Id" data-loaded="@(item.Text is not null ? "true" : "false")"
                                 tabindex="0" role="button"
                                 @onclick="@(() => OnExplore(item))" @onkeydown="@(e => OnItemKeyDown(e, item))">
                                @if (item.Text is { } text)
                                {
                                    <ScriptureRefText Text="@text" Toc="@(_toc ?? EmptyToc)" ClassPrefix="kretzmann-ref"
                                                       OwnerSlug="@Slug(item.Id)" OnExplore="OnExploreNode" />
                                }
                                else
                                {
                                    <span class="kretzmann-item-loading" aria-hidden="true">&hellip;</span>
                                }
                            </div>
                        }
                    </section>
                }
            }

            <nav class="kretzmann-nav" aria-label="Chapter navigation">
                @if (PrevTarget is { } prev)
                {
                    <button type="button" class="kretzmann-nav-button" data-testid="kretzmann-prev"
                            aria-label="@($"Previous chapter: {prev.Name} {prev.Chapter}")"
                            @onclick="@(() => OnChapterNavClick(prev.Book, prev.Chapter))">&lsaquo; @prev.Name @prev.Chapter</button>
                }
                @if (NextTarget is { } next)
                {
                    <button type="button" class="kretzmann-nav-button" data-testid="kretzmann-next"
                            aria-label="@($"Next chapter: {next.Name} {next.Chapter}")"
                            @onclick="@(() => OnChapterNavClick(next.Book, next.Chapter))">@next.Name @next.Chapter &rsaquo;</button>
                }
            </nav>
        </article>
    </div>
</CompositionSplit>

@if (_activeNode is not null)
{
    <ExplorerPopover @key="_activeNode" Root="_activeNode" OnClose="ClosePopover" />
}

@code {
    // Fix round (S-1): IsSectionStart is computed ONCE, structurally, when
    // the listing resolves (LoadCommentaryAsync below) -- true exactly when
    // this item's own Heading differs from the immediately preceding item's
    // in canonical order (mirrors Concord.RenderRows's own part/article
    // change-detection). `with { Text = ... }` (the lazy-load update path,
    // UpdateItemText below) preserves it untouched -- a later prose arrival
    // never affects section-heading placement.
    private sealed record CommentaryItemRow(string Id, string Heading, string? Text, bool IsSectionStart);
    private sealed record CommentaryVerseGroup(int Verse, List<CommentaryItemRow> Items);

    private static readonly List<BookTocEntry> EmptyToc = new();

    [Parameter] public bool? SplitMode { get; set; }

    private CompositionSplit? _compositionSplit;

    private Projection<Locus>? _locusBacking;
    private Projection<Locus> _locus => _locusBacking ??= new Projection<Locus>(LocusAtom);

    // Batch CORPREAD-1b (deliverable 0a/ticket K, THE FOLLOW-RELEASE LAW):
    // true whenever this view's own display should track the shared Locus
    // atom directly -- ALWAYS true while not split (nothing to escape from
    // standalone, see this file's own header), otherwise the shared
    // ViewArrangement's own Follow flag. Reads `ViewArrangementAtom.Value`
    // directly (the SAME shape World.razor's own pre-existing, allowlisted
    // `_follow` property already uses -- ConformanceTests.cs's own
    // NoComponentHeldArrangementState_NoRealSiteOutsideTheSanctionedOwners
    // gained a matching new entry for this file, reasoned the identical way).
    private bool Following => !(_compositionSplit?.IsSplitOpen ?? false) || ViewArrangementAtom.Value.Follow;

    // View-local, RELEASED-only browsing position -- plain scalars (never
    // typed as Locus), seeded from the shared atom's own value at the
    // INSTANT of release (ToggleFollowClick below). Null whenever following
    // (the shared atom is the live source then; these are simply unused).
    private string? _releasedBook;
    private int? _releasedChapter;

    private string _book => Following ? _locus.Value.Book : (_releasedBook ?? _locus.Value.Book);
    private int _chapterNum => Following ? _locus.Value.Chapter : (_releasedChapter ?? _locus.Value.Chapter);

    private List<BookTocEntry>? _toc;
    private List<CommentaryVerseGroup> _rows = new();
    private bool _loading = true;
    private string? _error;
    private int _requestId;
    private IExplorable? _activeNode;
    private IEscapeHatch? _followHatchBacking;

    private string? _loadedBook;
    private int? _loadedChapterNum;

    // Fix round (Q-2, CRITICAL): the lazy-prose mitigation's own state --
    // see this file's own header and wwwroot/js/lazyProse.js.
    private IJSObjectReference? _lazyJs;
    private DotNetObjectReference<Kretzmann>? _selfRef;
    private readonly HashSet<string> _proseRequested = new(); // one fetch per item, ever, per chapter generation
    private readonly SemaphoreSlim _proseFetchGate = new(8); // bounded ACTUAL concurrency, independent of when a fetch is requested

    private string BookName => _toc?.FirstOrDefault(b => b.Code == _book)?.Name ?? _book;

    // Wire ids ("CommentaryItem:kretzmann/0.1.0") carry ':' and '/' -- not
    // legal in a bare CSS-identifier-ish testid; a stable, readable slug for
    // the testid only (never used to re-derive the id itself).
    private static string Slug(string wireId) => wireId.Replace(':', '-').Replace('/', '-').Replace('.', '-');

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        // Batch CORP-1: the one legitimate direct LocusAtom.Changed
        // subscription this view needs -- see ConformanceTests.cs's own
        // EffectsOnlyViaRegistry allowlist entry for the full justification
        // (mirrors World.razor's own OnLocusChanged: pure re-render, plus
        // here a refetch, no JS interop/Materialize-shaped side effect).
        LocusAtom.Changed += OnLocusChanged;
        _selfRef = DotNetObjectReference.Create(this);

        try
        {
            _toc = await Atlas.Books();
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // Non-fatal -- BookName falls back to the bare code (see its own
            // property above); the commentary fetch below does not need the
            // TOC at all except to resolve THIS chapter's own verse count
            // and the scripture-ref scanner's own book-name alternation
            // (ScriptureRefText simply renders no explorable refs until the
            // TOC resolves -- Scan's own toc.Count==0 short-circuit).
        }

        await LoadCommentaryAsync();
    }

    // Fix round 2 (re-review finding, IMPORTANT -- CompositionSplit.razor's
    // own RULING 4(a), THE VC-1 hazard class this codebase documents
    // verbatim: "bind on EVERY render (guarded only by 'is my JS module
    // loaded,' never `firstRender`), relying on the JS side being
    // IDEMPOTENT and SELF-CLEANING"). Fix round 1's own first cut of this
    // method gated the rebind behind a `_pendingObserve` flag set ONLY
    // inside `LoadCommentaryAsync` -- i.e. only on a genuinely NEW chapter
    // load -- which is exactly the anti-pattern that ruling warns against:
    // this view's entire body (every `[data-kretzmann-item-id]` element the
    // observer watches) renders as `CompositionSplit`'s own `ChildContent`,
    // and toggling split open/closed on an UNCHANGED chapter is a
    // structurally different render tree (CompositionSplit.razor:126-151) --
    // Blazor discards and recreates every node inside `ChildContent` across
    // that transition. `OnArrangementChanged` below only calls
    // `LoadCommentaryAsync` (the sole place that used to set
    // `_pendingObserve = true`) when the book/chapter actually changed;
    // toggling split without navigating left the pre-toggle
    // `IntersectionObserver` instance watching now-detached nodes,
    // permanently stalling any not-yet-requested item's own prose fetch
    // until the reader changed chapters. Fixed the SAME way
    // `Reader.razor`'s own `watchScroll`/`watchChapterNavCenter` already
    // fix the identical hazard class (that file's own `OnAfterRenderAsync`
    // comment has the full incident history, NAV-3/NAV-6): rebind
    // UNCONDITIONALLY, every render, guarded only by "is my JS module
    // loaded" (`_lazyJs is not null`) -- relying entirely on
    // `lazyProse.js`'s own `observeProseItems` already being idempotent/
    // self-cleaning (`unobserveProseItems()` at its own top, see that
    // file's own header) to make a repeat call safe and cheap. A duplicate
    // callback for an item already requested is a guaranteed no-op on the
    // C# side too (`OnItemNearViewport`'s own `_proseRequested.Add` guard
    // below) -- re-observing an already-loaded or already-in-flight item
    // fires at most one more harmless, immediately-discarded invocation,
    // never a duplicate fetch.
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (firstRender)
        {
            try
            {
                _lazyJs = await JS.InvokeAsync<IJSObjectReference>("import", "./js/lazyProse.js");
            }
            catch (JSDisconnectedException)
            {
                return; // navigated away mid-import -- nothing left to bind
            }
        }

        if (_lazyJs is null)
        {
            return;
        }

        try
        {
            await _lazyJs.InvokeVoidAsync("observeProseItems", _selfRef, "[data-kretzmann-item-id]");
        }
        catch (JSDisconnectedException)
        {
            // Navigated away mid-call -- nothing left to observe.
        }
    }

    private void OnLocusChanged()
    {
        // Batch CORPREAD-1b, THE FOLLOW-RELEASE LAW: while released, this
        // view browses independently -- a shared-atom move (e.g. the
        // guest reader pane navigating) is deliberately IGNORED, exactly
        // the ticket's own text ("released Kretzmann browses independently").
        if (!Following)
        {
            return;
        }

        _activeNode = null; // a new chapter invalidates whatever was open, same reset Reader.razor already performs on navigation
        _ = LoadCommentaryAsync();
    }

    // Batch CORPREAD-1b: re-renders for the follow-chip's own live label,
    // and reconverges the loaded chapter whenever a split-close (or any
    // other arrangement change) flips `Following` back to true onto a
    // DIFFERENT locus than what is currently loaded (e.g. the guest reader
    // pane moved the shared atom while this view was released, then the
    // split closed).
    private void OnArrangementChanged()
    {
        if (_loadedBook != _book || _loadedChapterNum != _chapterNum)
        {
            _releasedBook = null;
            _releasedChapter = null;
            _activeNode = null;
            _ = LoadCommentaryAsync();
        }
        else
        {
            StateHasChanged();
        }
    }

    private async Task ToggleFollowClick()
    {
        var wasFollowing = Following;
        if (wasFollowing)
        {
            // RELEASE: seed the view-local position from the CURRENT shared
            // value -- deliverable 0b, verbatim: "released = view-local
            // position seeded from the shared value at release."
            _releasedBook = _locus.Value.Book;
            _releasedChapter = _locus.Value.Chapter;
        }

        _followHatchBacking ??= Registry.Get(ViewNames.Kretzmann).EscapeHatches.Single(h => h.Kind == HatchKinds.ToggleFollow);
        await _followHatchBacking.Invoke();

        if (wasFollowing)
        {
            StateHasChanged(); // released -- book/chapter identical at this instant, only the chip's own label changes
        }
        else
        {
            // RE-FOLLOW: reconverge -- deliverable 0b, verbatim: "re-follow
            // = reconverge." Drops the seed and reloads from whatever the
            // shared atom holds NOW, never a stale remembered position.
            _releasedBook = null;
            _releasedChapter = null;
            _activeNode = null;
            await LoadCommentaryAsync();
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        LocusAtom.Changed -= OnLocusChanged;

        if (_lazyJs is not null)
        {
            try
            {
                await _lazyJs.InvokeVoidAsync("unobserveProseItems");
                await _lazyJs.DisposeAsync();
            }
            catch (JSDisconnectedException)
            {
                // The circuit is already gone during teardown -- nothing left to clean up JS-side.
            }
        }

        _selfRef?.Dispose();
    }

    // ScripturePicker's own composed sref ("GEN.1", "GEN.1.5", "GEN.1.5-9")
    // -- Kretzmann only cares about book+chapter (R2's own "browses by
    // chapter" scope); any verse-range suffix is simply not read.
    private void ApplyScriptureRef(string sref)
    {
        var parts = sref.Split('.');
        if (parts.Length == 0 || string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(parts[0]))
        {
            return;
        }

        var book = parts[0];
        var chapter = parts.Length > 1 && int.TryParse(parts[1], out var c) ? c : 1;
        NavigateTo(book, chapter);
    }

    private sealed record AdjacentChapter(string Book, string Name, int Chapter);

    private AdjacentChapter? PrevTarget => ComputeAdjacent(forward: false);
    private AdjacentChapter? NextTarget => ComputeAdjacent(forward: true);

    // Batch CORPREAD-1b (ticket K, "chapter-to-chapter continuation ...
    // reads like the Bible reader's own flow"): mirrors Reader.razor's own
    // ComputeAdjacent exactly (crosses book boundaries via the TOC; null
    // -- hidden -- at GEN.1's prev and REV.22's next).
    private AdjacentChapter? ComputeAdjacent(bool forward)
    {
        if (_toc is null)
        {
            return null;
        }

        var idx = _toc.FindIndex(b => b.Code == _book);
        if (idx < 0)
        {
            return null;
        }

        var entry = _toc[idx];

        // Fix (found live via KRETZMANN-11's own real Playwright run, not a
        // guess): `BookTocEntry.Chapters` is each chapter's own VERSE COUNT
        // (e.g. Genesis's `[31,25,24,...]`), not a list of chapter NUMBERS
        // -- an earlier draft's `entry.Chapters.IndexOf(_chapterNum)` was
        // searching for "a chapter whose verse count equals the current
        // chapter NUMBER," a category error that silently misrouted every
        // chapter turn once past chapter ~15 or so (wherever a verse count
        // happened to coincide with a chapter number by accident). Corrected
        // to mirror Reader.razor's own real, working ComputeAdjacent exactly
        // (read directly before rewriting this, not re-guessed): chapter
        // numbers run 1..entry.Chapters.Count; `entry.Chapters.Count` alone
        // is what's needed, never an index lookup INTO the verse-count list.
        if (forward)
        {
            if (_chapterNum < entry.Chapters.Count)
            {
                return new AdjacentChapter(_book, entry.Name, _chapterNum + 1);
            }
            return idx + 1 < _toc.Count ? new AdjacentChapter(_toc[idx + 1].Code, _toc[idx + 1].Name, 1) : null;
        }

        if (_chapterNum > 1)
        {
            return new AdjacentChapter(_book, entry.Name, _chapterNum - 1);
        }
        if (idx == 0)
        {
            return null;
        }

        var prevEntry = _toc[idx - 1];
        return new AdjacentChapter(prevEntry.Code, prevEntry.Name, prevEntry.Chapters.Count);
    }

    private void OnChapterNavClick(string book, int chapterNum) => NavigateTo(book, chapterNum);

    // Batch CORPREAD-1b, THE FOLLOW-RELEASE LAW: the ONE choke point every
    // navigation gesture (picker, prev/next) funnels through -- while
    // FOLLOWING, dispatches onto the shared Locus atom (moves a guest
    // reader pane too, if one is mounted -- the pre-existing, unchanged
    // "free win" behavior); while RELEASED, writes the view-local scalars
    // directly and reloads locally, touching LocusAtom not at all (ticket
    // K, verbatim: "released Kretzmann browses independently").
    private void NavigateTo(string book, int chapterNum)
    {
        if (Following)
        {
            LocusAtom.Dispatch(new SetLocus(book, chapterNum));
        }
        else
        {
            _releasedBook = book;
            _releasedChapter = chapterNum;
            _activeNode = null;
            _ = LoadCommentaryAsync();
        }
    }

    private async Task LoadCommentaryAsync()
    {
        var myRequest = ++_requestId;
        var book = _book;
        var chapterNum = _chapterNum;
        _loading = true;
        _error = null;
        StateHasChanged();

        try
        {
            // KRETZ-SCALE-1: ONE chapter-scoped fetch for the LISTING
            // (headings, already grouped/ordered server-side) -- unchanged
            // from CORP-1's own shipped mechanism.
            var doc = await Atlas.KretzmannChapter(book, chapterNum);

            if (myRequest != _requestId)
            {
                return; // superseded by a newer navigation -- discard this stale response
            }

            // Fix round (S-1, CRITICAL): IsSectionStart computed HERE,
            // structurally, once -- true exactly when this item's own
            // Heading differs from the immediately preceding item's, in
            // canonical order, across verse boundaries (mirrors
            // Concord.RenderRows's own part/article change-detection).
            // `lastHeading` is a local, not view state -- correct by
            // construction for a fresh chapter load; a NEW LoadCommentaryAsync
            // call always starts it fresh, never carrying a stale heading
            // over from the previous chapter.
            string? lastHeading = null;
            var headingRows = new List<CommentaryVerseGroup>();
            foreach (var v in doc.Verses)
            {
                var items = new List<CommentaryItemRow>();
                foreach (var item in v.Items)
                {
                    var heading = item.Heading ?? "Commentary";
                    var isSectionStart = heading != lastHeading;
                    lastHeading = heading;
                    items.Add(new CommentaryItemRow(item.Id, heading, null, isSectionStart));
                }
                headingRows.Add(new CommentaryVerseGroup(v.Verse, items));
            }

            _rows = headingRows;
            _loadedBook = book;
            _loadedChapterNum = chapterNum;
            _loading = false;
            _proseRequested.Clear(); // fresh chapter -- a fresh lazy-load generation
            StateHasChanged(); // headings visible immediately -- prose loads lazily below, never blocking the whole chapter; OnAfterRenderAsync rebinds the observer unconditionally, every render (fix round 2), so no explicit "please re-observe" flag is needed here
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            if (myRequest != _requestId)
            {
                return;
            }

            _error = "Couldn't load the commentary for this chapter — check your connection and try again.";
            _loading = false;
            StateHasChanged();
        }
    }

    // Fix round (Q-2, CRITICAL): called from wwwroot/js/lazyProse.js's own
    // IntersectionObserver, once per item, the instant that item's own row
    // approaches the viewport -- never for the whole chapter up front (the
    // regression this fix exists to remove; see this file's own header).
    // `_proseRequested` is the authoritative "at most once" guard (the JS
    // side's own unobserve-on-trigger is a courtesy, not load-bearing);
    // `_proseFetchGate` bounds ACTUAL CONCURRENT fetches to 8 regardless of
    // how many items request one in a burst (e.g. a fast scroll that brings
    // many rows into range at once).
    [JSInvokable]
    public async Task OnItemNearViewport(string itemId)
    {
        if (!_proseRequested.Add(itemId))
        {
            return; // already requested (or a duplicate observer callback) -- fetch at most once per item per chapter generation
        }

        var myRequest = _requestId; // pins the chapter generation this request belongs to
        await _proseFetchGate.WaitAsync();
        try
        {
            if (myRequest != _requestId)
            {
                return; // superseded by a newer chapter navigation -- discard
            }

            string? text;
            try
            {
                var card = await Graph.Card(itemId);
                text = card.Description;
            }
            catch (Exception)
            {
                text = null; // fail soft -- the heading still renders, matches the app's own lazy-fetch degradation policy
            }

            if (myRequest != _requestId)
            {
                return;
            }

            UpdateItemText(itemId, text);
            StateHasChanged();
        }
        finally
        {
            _proseFetchGate.Release();
        }
    }

    // Fix round (Q-9 trivia, resolved as a side effect of the Q-2 rewrite):
    // each item's own prose fetch is now independently try/caught in
    // OnItemNearViewport above -- a failure for ONE item can no longer
    // discard the whole already-rendered chapter the way the old single
    // outer catch could (a duplicate-id ToDictionary failure, e.g.).
    private void UpdateItemText(string itemId, string? text)
    {
        _rows = _rows
            .Select(g => g.Items.Any(i => i.Id == itemId)
                ? g with { Items = g.Items.Select(i => i.Id == itemId ? i with { Text = text ?? i.Heading } : i).ToList() }
                : g)
            .ToList();
    }

    private void OnExplore(CommentaryItemRow item) => _activeNode = new CommentaryItemNode(item.Id, item.Heading);

    private void OnItemKeyDown(KeyboardEventArgs e, CommentaryItemRow item)
    {
        if (e.Key == "Enter")
        {
            OnExplore(item);
        }
    }

    // Batch CORPREAD-1b (ticket K, "the verse anchors themselves are
    // clickable explorables"): the SAME VerseNode every other verse
    // reference in this app opens (Reader's own verse-num click, xref
    // targets, ScriptureRefText's own in-prose matches below) -- ONE-RULE,
    // no bespoke anchor popover.
    private void OnExploreVerse(int verse) => _activeNode = new VerseNode($"{_book}.{_chapterNum}.{verse}");

    private void OnExploreNode(IExplorable node) => _activeNode = node;

    private void ClosePopover() => _activeNode = null;
}
